<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

    <!--
      Spec §16, guardrail 1: "Visible, user-initiated, foreground operation only.
      The agent runs as a foreground service with a persistent notification. No
      covert/background operation. No hiding the service."

      FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE is the correct type: AXON is not media,
      location or sync. Android requires a declared justification for it, given
      below, which is exactly the kind of visible accountability §16 asks for.

      Note what is NOT requested here, and deliberately so (§6.3, §16):
        • no INTERNET — enforced architecturally, not by policy. The perception
          and planner paths physically cannot exfiltrate screen contents because
          the app has no network permission at all. That turns "we don't upload
          your screen" from a promise into a property an examiner can verify from
          the manifest.
        • no RECEIVE_SMS / READ_SMS, no ACCESS_BACKGROUND_LOCATION, no
          QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES — the stalkerware-adjacent set.
    -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.FOREGROUND_SERVICE_SPECIAL_USE" />
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

    <!--
      Package visibility, scoped to what the user can already see (E21c).

      Android 11+ hides installed packages from an app unless it declares what it
      needs. AXON declared nothing, which was not a decision — it was an
      omission, and it silently disabled a feature: `PackageAppResolver` resolves
      "whatsapp" to com.whatsapp by reading the launcher's list, and with no
      visibility that list is empty. So E21's grammar collapse — the strongest
      constraint in the system, narrowing the model to exactly one legal action —
      never fired on device. It passed every unit test and was inert on hardware.

      The fix is NOT QUERY_ALL_PACKAGES. That permission reveals every installed
      package including the non-launchable ones — services, providers, things the
      user has no icon for — which is what makes it stalkerware-adjacent, why
      §6.3 excludes it, and why Google Play restricts it.

      This <queries> element is strictly narrower: it grants visibility of apps
      that have a launcher icon, and nothing else. That is precisely the set the
      user already sees on their home screen and can already tap. AXON learns
      nothing about the device that its owner cannot learn by looking at it.

      Scoping visibility explicitly is a better §16 story than the accidental
      version it replaces, where the restriction held by omission and the cost
      was a feature failing silently.
    -->
    <queries>
        <intent>
            <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
            <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
        </intent>
    </queries>

    <application
        android:allowBackup="false"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:label="@string/app_name"
        android:supportsRtl="true"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.Axon">

        <activity
            android:name=".MainActivity"
            android:exported="true"
            android:label="@string/app_name">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>
        </activity>

        <!--
          The gateway (§7.9): a foreground service other clients talk to, which
          is what makes AXON "a service, not an app". Wired in Phase 6; declared
          here so the permission model and the notification are in place from the
          start rather than retrofitted onto a working agent.
        -->
        <service
            android:name=".gateway.AxonGatewayService"
            android:exported="true"
            android:foregroundServiceType="specialUse">
            <property
                android:name="android.app.PROPERTY_SPECIAL_USE_FGS_SUBTYPE"
                android:value="On-device accessibility agent performing user-requested
                    device tasks. Runs only while a task is in progress and shows a
                    persistent notification for its entire lifetime." />
        </service>

    </application>

</manifest>
